{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":23015,"duration_api_ms":24172,"num_turns":10,"result":"I wrote `src/48-energy/cpufreq.bt`, but I haven't run it. I couldn't check whether bpftrace is installed here, and it needs root to run.\n\nIt attaches to the `power:cpu_frequency` tracepoint, which fires on each CPU frequency change. It prints a live line for each transition with the time, CPU, old frequency and new frequency in MHz. On Ctrl-C it prints:\n- `@transitions`: the number of changes per CPU.\n- `@time_ms`: milliseconds spent at each frequency, per CPU.\n- `@freq_mhz`: a histogram of the new frequencies.\n\nTwo limitations:\n- The first event on each CPU shows an old frequency of 0, because there is no earlier value to compare against.\n- `@time_ms` leaves out the last interval, from the final transition to Ctrl-C.\n\nTo run it: `sudo bpftrace src/48-energy/cpufreq.bt`\n\nThe tracepoint only fires when the cpufreq driver actually changes frequency. On some systems, such as intel_pstate with hardware-managed P-states, it fires rarely or not at all. If the output stays empty, I can add a version that polls `/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq`.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"57e81a3d-61b3-420a-a363-be216d22ebd6","total_cost_usd":0.09681880000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3500,"cache_read_input_tokens":157986,"output_tokens":2387,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3500},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":427,"cache_read_input_tokens":28065,"cache_creation_input_tokens":716,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":716,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":351,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens"